  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 328 AM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nBeyond Thursday night, strong upper level ridging will return to the \nwestern US allowing for surface high pressure to build south out of \nCanada bringing a return to dry conditions through the weekend. With \nthis high, cold air advection will push in a much colder airmass \ninto the northern and central Great Plains. With 850 mb temperatures \nin the -1 to 4 C range on Friday, surface highs will struggle to \nrise out of the 50s. Highs will gradually moderate through the \nweekend, rising back into the 70s by Sunday. This milder, near \nnormal air will persist through the beginning of next week.\n\nFor low temperatures, continued CAA into the region will result in \nwidespread low temperatures in the low to mid 30s continue into \nFriday night/Saturday morning. Some locations across southwest \nNebraska may even drop into the upper 20s. Given this colder \nairmass, frost and freeze potential is likely again on Saturday \nmorning. Similar to Friday morning, headlines are not in place, but \nif this colder temperature trend remains consistent and confidence \nincreases, they will likely be necessary. Stay tuned to the latest \nforecasts for the most up to date information regarding frost/freeze \npotential. Similar to high temperatures, overnight lows will also \nbegin to rise beyond Saturday returning to the 40s through the \nbeginning of next week. Therefore, beyond Friday and Saturday \nmorning, frost/freeze is not expected across our forecast area.",
